{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-007-v3","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"A","working":"The capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor is C = ε₀A/d. For circular plates, A = πr². Thus, A₂ = π(2R)² = 4πR² = 4A₁ and d₂ = 3d. Therefore, C₂/C₁ = (A₂/d₂) / (A₁/d₁) = (4/3) / (1/1) = 4/3.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.3","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10701,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10701,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":476,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":190,"reasoningTokens":286},"totalTokens":11177,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":286,"promptTokenCount":10701,"candidatesTokenCount":190,"totalTokenCount":11177,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10701}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":190}]},"reasoningTokens":286,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":4466,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-006-v3","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"D","working":"The relationship between electric field and potential in one dimension is E_x = -dV/dx. At local extrema of V(x), dV/dx = 0, so E_x = 0 at both points. Between a local maximum and a subsequent local minimum with no other stationary points, V decreases, so dV/dx < 0, which means E_x > 0.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.2","required_units":[9],"difficulty":"Medium"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10748,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10748,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":775,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":177,"reasoningTokens":598},"totalTokens":11523,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":598,"promptTokenCount":10748,"candidatesTokenCount":177,"totalTokenCount":11523,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10748}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":177}]},"reasoningTokens":598,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":5567,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-001-v3","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"By Gauss's law, the net electric flux through any closed surface is determined solely by the enclosed charge: Phi_E = q_enc / epsilon_0. Since the inside charge remains enclosed and the outside charge remains outside, q_enc is unchanged (+4.0 microCoulombs), so the net electric flux stays the same.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"8.6","required_units":[8],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10748,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10748,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":591,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":164,"reasoningTokens":427},"totalTokens":11339,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":427,"promptTokenCount":10748,"candidatesTokenCount":164,"totalTokenCount":11339,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10748}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":164}]},"reasoningTokens":427,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":5711,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-007-v3","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"A","working":"For a vacuum parallel-plate capacitor, C = ε₀A/d. With circular plates, A = πr². C₁ = ε₀πR²/d. C₂ = ε₀π(2R)²/(3d) = ε₀π4R²/(3d). Ratio C₂/C₁ = (4R²/(3d))/(R²/d) = 4/3.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.3 Capacitors","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10299,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":59,"cacheReadTokens":10240},"outputTokens":653,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":173,"reasoningTokens":480},"totalTokens":10952,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10299,"completion_tokens":653,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10240,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":59,"total_tokens":10952,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10240},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":480}},"reasoningTokens":480,"cachedInputTokens":10240},"ms":5824,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-001-v3","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"Gauss's law gives net flux through a closed surface depending only on enclosed charge. Moving the enclosed charge inside the surface does not change the enclosed charge; moving an outside charge closer still leaves it outside, so its net contribution is zero. Enclosed charge remains +4.0 μC, so flux remains the same.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"8.6 Gauss's Law","required_units":[8],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10348,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":108,"cacheReadTokens":10240},"outputTokens":735,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":150,"reasoningTokens":585},"totalTokens":11083,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10348,"completion_tokens":735,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10240,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":108,"total_tokens":11083,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10240},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":585}},"reasoningTokens":585,"cachedInputTokens":10240},"ms":7454,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-005-v3","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"The electric field along the x-axis is related to electric potential by E_x = -dV/dx. For 0 < x < 0.20 m, dV/dx = (2 - 10)/(0.20 - 0) = -40 V/m, so E_x = +40 V/m. For 0.20 m < x < 0.50 m, dV/dx = (11 - 2)/(0.50 - 0.20) = +30 V/m, so E_x = -30 V/m.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.2","required_units":[9],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10836,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10836,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":1010,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":230,"reasoningTokens":780},"totalTokens":11846,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":780,"promptTokenCount":10836,"candidatesTokenCount":230,"totalTokenCount":11846,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10836}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":230}]},"reasoningTokens":780,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":7528,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-025-v2","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"By Gauss's law, the net electric flux through a closed surface depends only on the enclosed charge: Φ_E = q_enc/ε₀. Because the charge is located outside the imaginary closed cube, q_enc = 0, so the net flux is zero (every field line entering the volume must exit).","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"8.6","required_units":[8],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10725,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10725,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":566,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":161,"reasoningTokens":405},"totalTokens":11291,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":405,"promptTokenCount":10725,"candidatesTokenCount":161,"totalTokenCount":11291,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10725}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":161}]},"reasoningTokens":405,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":5001,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-025-v3","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"Gauss's law gives net flux through a closed surface based only on enclosed charge. The outside −Q does not change the enclosed +Q, so net flux remains +Q/ε0. But the outside charge does change the electric field at surface points. Therefore C is correct.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"8.6 Gauss's Law","required_units":[8],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10324,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":84,"cacheReadTokens":10240},"outputTokens":586,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":142,"reasoningTokens":444},"totalTokens":10910,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10324,"completion_tokens":586,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10240,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":84,"total_tokens":10910,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10240},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":444}},"reasoningTokens":444,"cachedInputTokens":10240},"ms":6025,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-025-v3","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"By Gauss's law, net electric flux depends only on enclosed charge, which remains +Q. However, the electric field at points on the surface depends on the superposition of fields from all charges, both inside and outside. Thus, net flux is unchanged, but the local electric field changes.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"8.6","required_units":[8],"difficulty":"Medium"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10724,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10724,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":735,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":153,"reasoningTokens":582},"totalTokens":11459,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":582,"promptTokenCount":10724,"candidatesTokenCount":153,"totalTokenCount":11459,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10724}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":153}]},"reasoningTokens":582,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":6304,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-025-v2","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"Gauss's law gives net flux through a closed surface as q_enc/ε0. The charge is outside the cube, so q_enc=0; therefore total flux is zero. Field lines entering the cube also leave it, giving no net outward flux.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"8.6 Gauss's Law","required_units":[8],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10326,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":214,"cacheReadTokens":10112},"outputTokens":738,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":139,"reasoningTokens":599},"totalTokens":11064,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10326,"completion_tokens":738,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10112,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":214,"total_tokens":11064,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10112},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":599}},"reasoningTokens":599,"cachedInputTokens":10112},"ms":7365,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-029-v1","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"D","working":"In electrostatic equilibrium, charges are at rest. A tangential component of the electric field would exert a force on free charges causing them to accelerate along the surface, violating equilibrium. Therefore, the tangential field must be zero, meaning the field just outside must be strictly perpendicular to the surface.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.1","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10715,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10715,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":611,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":152,"reasoningTokens":459},"totalTokens":11326,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":459,"promptTokenCount":10715,"candidatesTokenCount":152,"totalTokenCount":11326,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10715}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":152}]},"reasoningTokens":459,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":5512,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-np1-006-v1","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"Using Gauss's law for a uniformly charged insulating sphere, the enclosed charge scales as r^3, while the Gaussian surface area scales as r^2. Thus, the internal field is directly proportional to r: E(r) = E_s(r/R). At r = R/3, E = E_s/3.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"8.6","required_units":[8],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10701,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10701,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":500,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":165,"reasoningTokens":335},"totalTokens":11201,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":335,"promptTokenCount":10701,"candidatesTokenCount":165,"totalTokenCount":11201,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10701}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":165}]},"reasoningTokens":335,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":4268,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-np1-003-v2","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"D","working":"The electric field along the x-axis is related to the electric potential by Ex = -dV/dx. In the region 2.0 m < x < 5.0 m, the potential changes linearly by ΔV = 4.0 V - 10 V = -6.0 V over Δx = 3.0 m, so dV/dx = -2.0 V/m. Thus, Ex = -(-2.0 V/m) = +2.0 V/m.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.2","required_units":[9],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10769,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10769,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":682,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":203,"reasoningTokens":479},"totalTokens":11451,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":479,"promptTokenCount":10769,"candidatesTokenCount":203,"totalTokenCount":11451,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10769}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":203}]},"reasoningTokens":479,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":5750,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-np1-003-v2","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"D","working":"From x=2.0 m to x=5.0 m, V drops from 10 V to 4 V, so ΔV = -6 V over Δx = 3.0 m. Thus dV/dx = -2 V/m. E_x = -dV/dx = 2 V/m. Since V decreases with increasing x, the field points in the +x direction. Answer D.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.2 Electric Potential","required_units":[9],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10363,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":123,"cacheReadTokens":10240},"outputTokens":1029,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":170,"reasoningTokens":859},"totalTokens":11392,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10363,"completion_tokens":1029,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10240,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":123,"total_tokens":11392,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10240},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":859}},"reasoningTokens":859,"cachedInputTokens":10240},"ms":9186,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-005-v3","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"Use E_x = -ΔV/Δx per segment. From x=0 to 0.20 m: -(2-10)/0.20 = +40 V/m. From x=0.20 to 0.50 m: -(11-2)/0.30 = -30 V/m. This matches B.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.2 Electric Potential","required_units":[8,9],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10407,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":39,"cacheReadTokens":10368},"outputTokens":2552,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":156,"reasoningTokens":2396},"totalTokens":12959,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10407,"completion_tokens":2552,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10368,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":39,"total_tokens":12959,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10368},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2396}},"reasoningTokens":2396,"cachedInputTokens":10368},"ms":22362,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-006-v3","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"D","working":"At local extrema dV/dx=0, so E_x=0 at both x=1 and x=3. Since V decreases from its maximum at 1 to its minimum at 3 as x increases, dV/dx<0 between them. With E_x=-dV/dx, E_x is positive there. Thus D.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"9.2 Electric Potential","required_units":[9],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10342,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":102,"cacheReadTokens":10240},"outputTokens":2487,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":134,"reasoningTokens":2353},"totalTokens":12829,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10342,"completion_tokens":2487,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10240,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":102,"total_tokens":12829,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10240},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2353}},"reasoningTokens":2353,"cachedInputTokens":10240},"ms":22660,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-029-v1","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"D","working":"At electrostatic equilibrium, mobile charges in a conductor are at rest. A tangential component of the surface field would exert a force on charges along the surface, producing a current and violating equilibrium. Thus the field must be perpendicular; D is correct. A is false, B assumes uniform charge (not generally true), and C invents a surface current.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.1","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10316,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":76,"cacheReadTokens":10240},"outputTokens":1844,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":151,"reasoningTokens":1693},"totalTokens":12160,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10316,"completion_tokens":1844,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10240,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":76,"total_tokens":12160,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10240},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1693}},"reasoningTokens":1693,"cachedInputTokens":10240},"ms":16465,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-np1-006-v1","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"B","working":"Inside a uniformly charged sphere, for r<R, Q_enc = Q(r^3/R^3). By Gauss's law, E = k Q_enc/r^2 = k Q r/R^3. At the surface, E_s = kQ/R^2. At r=R/3, E = kQ(R/3)/R^3 = kQ/(3R^2) = E_s/3.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"8.6 Gauss's Law","required_units":[8],"difficulty":"Medium"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10296,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":56,"cacheReadTokens":10240},"outputTokens":806,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":153,"reasoningTokens":653},"totalTokens":11102,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10296,"completion_tokens":806,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10240,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":56,"total_tokens":11102,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10240},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":653}},"reasoningTokens":653,"cachedInputTokens":10240},"ms":7293,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-030-v1","model":"deepseek/deepseek-v4-pro","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"The battery is disconnected, so charge Q on the plates is constant. Inserting the dielectric increases capacitance from C0 to κC0 = 4C0. Stored energy is U = Q²/(2C), so U_f = Q²/(2·4C0) = (1/4)U0.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.4 Dielectrics","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Medium"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10298,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":58,"cacheReadTokens":10240},"outputTokens":2368,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":152,"reasoningTokens":2216},"totalTokens":12666,"raw":{"prompt_tokens":10298,"completion_tokens":2368,"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":10240,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":58,"total_tokens":12666,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":10240},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2216}},"reasoningTokens":2216,"cachedInputTokens":10240},"ms":22525,"error":""}
{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-030-v1","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","sample":1,"ok":true,"label":{"chosen_label":"C","working":"Since the battery is disconnected, the charge Q remains constant. Inserting the dielectric increases capacitance to C = κC_0 = 4C_0. Using U = Q²/(2C), the stored energy becomes U = Q²/(2 · 4C_0) = U_0/4, which is one-fourth of its original value.","more_than_one_defensible_choice":false,"scope_verdict":"fully_in_scope","out_of_scope_concepts":[],"primary_topic_code":"10.4","required_units":[10],"difficulty":"Easy"},"usage":{"inputTokens":10699,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":10699,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":586,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":170,"reasoningTokens":416},"totalTokens":11285,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":416,"promptTokenCount":10699,"candidatesTokenCount":170,"totalTokenCount":11285,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":10699}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":170}]},"reasoningTokens":416,"cachedInputTokens":0},"ms":292204,"error":""}
